---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";

const title = "Del Norte Project Approved: What West Covina Residents Need to Know | StayJustIn";
const description =
  "West Covina approved the Del Norte project with 232 homes, park land dedication, park fees, first-time homebuyer assistance, and ongoing questions about traffic, Little League fields, and city costs.";
---

<MainLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="https://www.stayjustin.com/development/del-norte-project-west-covina/"
>
  <section class="article-page">
    <div class="container article-container">
      <p class="page-kicker">Development Watch</p>

      <h1>Del Norte Project Approved: What West Covina Residents Need to Know</h1>

      <p class="article-dek">
        232 homes, 1.45 acres of park land, nearly $1 million in park fees,
        and major questions about Little League, traffic, and long-term city costs.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <p>
        After months of public meetings, revisions, neighborhood concerns, and debate
        over parks, traffic, housing density, and Little League fields, the West Covina
        City Council approved the Del Norte residential development project on
        <strong>June 16, 2026</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The approval allows construction of <strong>232 homes</strong> on the former
        Del Norte School property at <strong>1501 W. Del Norte Street</strong>, while
        also requiring park land dedication, park fees, and first-time homebuyer
        assistance contributions.
      </p>

      <div class="info-box">
        <h2>Executive Summary</h2>

        <h3>Approved Project Includes</h3>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li>232 residential units</li>
          <li>34 detached single-family homes</li>
          <li>198 attached townhomes</li>
          <li>1.45 acres of park land dedicated to the City</li>
          <li>$961,350 park fee payment</li>
          <li>Up to $250,000 in field improvements</li>
          <li>First-time homebuyer assistance funding</li>
          <li>New roadway connections and site infrastructure</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Major Questions Remain</h3>
        <ul class="question-list">
          <li>Will the baseball field function as a true replacement field?</li>
          <li>Where will the $961,350 park fee ultimately be spent?</li>
          <li>Has Del Norte been studied together with the City Yard proposal?</li>
          <li>What long-term maintenance costs will the City inherit?</li>
          <li>Will the homes be affordable to most West Covina families?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>What Is the Del Norte Project?</h2>

      <p>
        The Del Norte project is a residential development proposed by
        <strong>Meritage Homes</strong> on the former Del Norte School property.
      </p>

      <p>
        The site consists of approximately <strong>13.6 acres</strong> located near
        Del Norte Street and Sunset Avenue, adjacent to Del Norte Park.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>232 residential units</li>
        <li>34 detached homes</li>
        <li>198 attached townhomes</li>
        <li>Private internal streets</li>
        <li>Community amenities</li>
        <li>New roadway connections</li>
        <li>Park dedication to the City</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How the Project Changed</h2>

      <p>
        The project residents saw approved was significantly different from the
        original proposal.
      </p>

      <div class="timeline-box">
        <div>
          <h3>Original Proposal</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>282 units</li>
            <li>Included four-story buildings</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Revised Proposal</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Reduced to 260 units</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Final Approved Version</h3>
          <ul class="check-list">
            <li>Reduced to 232 units</li>
            <li>Four-story buildings removed</li>
            <li>Increased park dedication</li>
            <li>Additional neighborhood compatibility measures</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Why the Project Was Proposed</h2>

      <p>
        A recurring theme throughout the discussion was California’s housing
        requirements.
      </p>

      <div class="stat-box">
        <strong>West Covina RHNA Requirement:</strong>
        <span>5,346 housing units</span>
      </div>

      <ul>
        <li>Approximately 511 units have been completed</li>
        <li>Roughly 4,835 units remain</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Supporters argued that the City must continue adding housing opportunities
        or risk potential consequences from the State. Opponents argued that
        compliance should not come at the expense of community amenities and
        neighborhood character.
      </p>

      <h2>The Housing Debate</h2>

      <p>
        One of the most discussed questions during the hearing was affordability:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        Who can actually afford these homes?
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        During the meeting, Councilmember Tony Wu asked the developer about
        anticipated pricing. Meritage Homes responded that based on current market
        conditions:
      </p>

      <div class="two-column">
        <div class="mini-card">
          <h3>Townhomes</h3>
          <p>Estimated starting prices in the <strong>mid-$600,000 range</strong>.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="mini-card">
          <h3>Detached Homes</h3>
          <p>Estimated starting prices in the <strong>mid-$800,000 range</strong>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Actual future prices remain unknown and will depend on market conditions
        when homes are sold.
      </p>

      <h2>First-Time Homebuyer Assistance</h2>

      <p>
        One change negotiated during the approval process involved increasing
        assistance for future homebuyers.
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>$25,000 per qualifying unit</li>
        <li>Up to 10% of project units</li>
        <li>Increased from the originally proposed $15,000 amount</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Baseball Field Debate</h2>

      <p>
        The baseball field became the most emotional issue discussed throughout
        the process.
      </p>

      <h3>What Was Approved</h3>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>1.45 acres dedicated to the City</li>
        <li>Land becomes part of Del Norte Park</li>
        <li>Field transferred after required improvements are completed</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Why Residents Objected</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>A larger field would better serve older age divisions</li>
        <li>The approved field may primarily accommodate younger players</li>
        <li>Recreational capacity could be reduced</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Developer Response</h3>

      <p>
        Meritage argued that expanding the field further would significantly affect:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Fire access</li>
        <li>Site circulation</li>
        <li>Parking</li>
        <li>Project design</li>
        <li>Housing layout</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Unresolved Question:</strong>
        Is the City receiving a true replacement field or a smaller facility serving
        fewer age groups?
      </div>

      <h2>Park Dedication and Park Fees</h2>

      <p>The project provides two separate park-related benefits.</p>

      <div class="two-column">
        <div class="mini-card">
          <h3>Park Land</h3>
          <p><strong>1.45 acres</strong> dedicated to the City of West Covina.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="mini-card">
          <h3>Park Fee</h3>
          <p><strong>$961,350</strong> park-in-lieu fee, commonly called a Quimby Fee.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Why the Fee Was Reduced</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>Original formula produced a fee approaching $2.5 million to $3 million</li>
        <li>Dedicated park land generated approximately $1.58 million in credit</li>
        <li>Final fee obligation became $961,350</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What Residents Want to Know</h3>

      <blockquote>
        Where will the money go?
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Councilmembers discussed possibilities including dog park improvements,
        Little League facilities, snack bar upgrades, and additional park improvements.
        At this time, no formal allocation has been approved.
      </p>

      <h2>Additional Field Improvements</h2>

      <p>
        Separate from the park fee, the City required the developer to spend up to:
      </p>

      <div class="stat-box">
        <strong>Field Improvement Requirement:</strong>
        <span>$250,000</span>
      </div>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Grading</li>
        <li>Irrigation</li>
        <li>Sod installation</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Traffic Concerns</h2>

      <p>Traffic remained one of the most frequently discussed concerns.</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Traffic studies were completed</li>
        <li>City traffic engineers participated</li>
        <li>The studies analyzed the larger 282-unit proposal</li>
        <li>The approved 232-unit project is smaller than what was studied</li>
        <li>A left-turn pocket from Sunset Avenue was added</li>
        <li>Fair-share transportation contributions will be required</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Despite those assurances, some residents remain concerned about neighborhood
        traffic impacts once the project is occupied.
      </p>

      <h2>The Question That Was Never Fully Answered</h2>

      <p>
        One of the most significant questions involved cumulative impacts.
      </p>

      <div class="stat-grid">
        <div>
          <strong>Del Norte</strong>
          <span>232 units</span>
        </div>

        <div>
          <strong>City Yard</strong>
          <span>141 units</span>
        </div>

        <div>
          <strong>Combined</strong>
          <span>373 units</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Residents asked whether the City had evaluated combined traffic, public
        safety, park, and infrastructure impacts. No direct answer to that specific
        question was provided during the discussion.
      </p>

      <h2>Parking Concerns</h2>

      <p>The project provides:</p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>526 total parking spaces</li>
        <li>Two-car garages for all units</li>
        <li>Guest parking</li>
        <li>Additional street parking opportunities</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        To address concerns, the Council added requirements that garages be used
        for vehicle storage, guest parking permits be implemented, and HOA rules
        reinforce parking requirements.
      </p>

      <h2>Final Vote</h2>

      <p>The project passed on a <strong>3-2 vote</strong>.</p>

      <div class="two-column">
        <div class="mini-card yes-card">
          <h3>In Support</h3>
          <ul class="check-list">
            <li>Mayor Letty Lopez-Viado</li>
            <li>Councilmember Rosario Diaz</li>
            <li>Councilmember Tony Wu</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mini-card no-card">
          <h3>In Opposition</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Mayor Pro Tem Ollie Cantos</li>
            <li>Councilmember Brian Gutierrez</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>What Happens Next?</h2>

      <p>Approval does not mean construction begins immediately.</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Final engineering</li>
        <li>Final tract map approvals</li>
        <li>Permit processing</li>
        <li>Site preparation</li>
        <li>Infrastructure installation</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Questions Residents Should Continue Asking</h2>

      <ol class="question-number-list">
        <li>Where will the $961,350 park fee ultimately be spent?</li>
        <li>How much will annual maintenance of the dedicated park land cost?</li>
        <li>Will the baseball field support the same age groups as before?</li>
        <li>Is the $250,000 field improvement requirement separate from the park fee?</li>
        <li>Has the City evaluated the combined impact of Del Norte and City Yard?</li>
        <li>How will traffic change once the project is occupied?</li>
        <li>Will the homes be affordable to typical West Covina families?</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Bottom Line</h2>

      <p>
        The Del Norte project represents one of the largest housing approvals in
        West Covina in recent years.
      </p>

      <p>
        The City gained new housing opportunities, additional park land, nearly
        $1 million in park fees, homebuyer assistance funding, and new infrastructure
        improvements.
      </p>

      <p>
        At the same time, residents continue raising questions about traffic, parking,
        recreational capacity, long-term maintenance costs, and combined development
        impacts.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        The vote may be complete. The questions are not.
      </div>

      <hr />

      <h2>Related Del Norte Coverage</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><a href="/development/del-norte-park-fee/">Where Does the Del Norte Park Fee Go?</a></li>
        <li><a href="/development/del-norte-field-debate/">The Del Norte Baseball Field Debate Explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/development/del-norte-city-yard-combined-impact/">Has West Covina Studied Del Norte and City Yard Together?</a></li>
        <li><a href="/development/del-norte-unanswered-questions/">The Questions Residents Are Still Asking</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .article-page {
      padding: 44px 0;
      background: #ffffff;
    }

    .article-container {
      max-width: 860px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      font-size: 1.15rem;
      line-height: 1.8;
    }

    .page-kicker {
      color: #d99a00;
      font-weight: 800;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    .article-container h1 {
      font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5vw, 3.8rem);
      line-height: 1.05;
      margin-bottom: 18px;
      color: #0b2f4a;
    }

    .article-dek {
      font-size: 1.35rem;
      line-height: 1.55;
      color: #334155;
      font-weight: 600;
      margin-bottom: 30px;
    }

    .article-container h2 {
      font-size: clamp(1.65rem, 4vw, 2.25rem);
      line-height: 1.15;
      color: #0b2f4a;
      margin-top: 44px;
      margin-bottom: 16px;
      padding-top: 12px;
      border-top: 3px solid #f0b323;
    }

    .article-container h3 {
      font-size: 1.25rem;
      color: #12344d;
      margin-top: 24px;
      margin-bottom: 8px;
    }

    .article-container p {
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    .article-container ul,
    .article-container ol {
      margin: 18px 0 24px;
      padding-left: 1.4rem;
    }

    .article-container li {
      margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    .check-list {
      list-style: none;
      padding-left: 0 !important;
    }

    .check-list li::before {
      content: "✅ ";
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .question-list {
      list-style: none;
      padding-left: 0 !important;
    }

    .question-list li::before {
      content: "❓ ";
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .question-number-list li {
      padding-left: 4px;
      font-weight: 600;
    }

    .info-box,
    .callout,
    .timeline-box,
    .mini-card,
    .stat-box {
      border-radius: 18px;
      border: 1px solid #dbeafe;
      background: #f8fbff;
      padding: 22px;
      margin: 28px 0;
      box-shadow: 0 10px 25px rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.06);
    }

    .info-box {
      border-left: 6px solid #f0b323;
    }

    .callout {
      background: #0b2f4a;
      color: #ffffff;
      border: none;
      font-size: 1.2rem;
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    blockquote {
      margin: 24px 0;
      padding: 18px 22px;
      border-left: 6px solid #f0b323;
      background: #fff7e6;
      color: #0b2f4a;
      font-size: 1.35rem;
      font-weight: 800;
    }

    .two-column,
    .stat-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 18px;
      margin: 26px 0;
    }

    .stat-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    .stat-grid div,
    .stat-box {
      background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0b2f4a, #0e7490);
      color: #ffffff;
      border: none;
      text-align: center;
    }

    .stat-box strong,
    .stat-grid strong {
      display: block;
      font-size: 1rem;
      color: #f0b323;
      margin-bottom: 8px;
    }

    .stat-box span,
    .stat-grid span {
      display: block;
      font-size: 2rem;
      font-weight: 900;
      line-height: 1.1;
    }

    .yes-card {
      border-left: 6px solid #22c55e;
    }

    .no-card {
      border-left: 6px solid #ef4444;
    }

    .article-container a {
      color: #0369a1;
      font-weight: 800;
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    hr {
      border: none;
      border-top: 1px solid #dbeafe;
      margin: 34px 0;
    }

    @media (max-width: 720px) {
      .article-page {
        padding: 28px 0;
      }

      .article-container {
        font-size: 1.08rem;
        line-height: 1.75;
      }

      .two-column,
      .stat-grid {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }

      .article-dek {
        font-size: 1.18rem;
      }

      .stat-box span,
      .stat-grid span {
        font-size: 1.65rem;
      }
    }
  </style>
</MainLayout>
